// @check
// @error cannot compare Id and null
// @hint redundant type annotation: `v` is inferred as `Id` — remove it to lean on inference
// THE INVERTED CONTROL for `alias-over-nullable-null-test.vl`'s second half. A newtype
// over a NON-nullable base admits no null, so `v != null` is a guard that can never be
// false — the reject `null-guard-on-non-nullable-rejects.vl` pins for un-branded types,
// held for a branded one.
//
// It is a real pin and not a restatement, because the `null`-operand exemption that made
// `new (string | null)` testable is read TWICE: once as `nomBinMixes` here, and once as
// the `nomEqLit` escape inside the equality arm. An UNGATED exemption (one that does not
// consult `tyAdmitsNull`) therefore does not merely soften this message — it suppresses
// the general `cannot compare … with null` reject too, and this program walks past the
// checker into `emitProgram: bare null needs a struct-typed context`. Measured, in
// exactly that shape, while the gate was absent.
type Id = new i32
const v: Id = 5 as Id
if v != null { print(v) } else { print(0) }
